{let n=Br(e.trim());return n.length<2?[]:(Gr??=Wr(),Gr.filter(e=>Br(e.text).includes(n)).slice(0,t).map(t=>({...t.hit,snippet:Vr(t.text,e.trim())})))}var qr={title:`Shadowbox`,home:`Home`,search:`Search`,searchPlaceholder:`Search the record`,menu:`Menu`,noMatch:`No match in the record.`,timeline:`Timeline`,logbook:`Logbook`,admin:`Admin`,commands:`Commands`,uniforms:`Uniforms`,decorations:`Decorations`,onduty:`On Duty`,offduty:`Off Duty`,ops:`Operations`,map:`Map`,schools:`Schools`,necs:`NECs`,dateNeeded:`Date needed`,sources:`Sources`,contact:`Contact`,guestbook:`Guestbook`,memories:`Memories`,settings:`Settings`,close:`Close`,made:`Made by Myself & Grok, 2026`,bio:`Chief Schickler enlisted in Miami on 30 June 1997 as a Seaman and retired on 28 February 2018 as an Electronics Technician after 20 years, 8 months, and 1 day. There was no service in the two apprentice grades.

His advancement through the petty officer grades was fast. Petty Officer Third Class in December 1998, Petty Officer Second Class in May 1999, and Petty Officer First Class in June 2003. Petty Officer Second Class came in under two years of service, when Navy-wide averages of that era put E-5 a little past five years. Petty Officer First Class came at six years, when those averages put E-6 past eleven, and when the Electronics Technician path still lists about nine years to that grade. Selection for chief petty officer came from Joint Communications Support Element, with a date of rank of 16 September 2014, in the last year of that tour.

Nearly half the career was overseas. From 2001 to 2007 the duty was Guam. On USS Frank Cable (AS-40) the primary duty was Fleet Electronics Calibration Technician, as Leading Petty Officer, and the collateral duty was Safety. Enlisted Surface Warfare Specialist was earned on that ship. Three Navy E Ribbons dated 2001, 2002, and 2003 are recorded for the ship, along with the Humanitarian Service Medal for Typhoon Pongsona, dated 2003, and the Navy Rifle Marksmanship Ribbon at Expert, recorded in 2001. At Explosive Ordnance Disposal Mobile Unit Five the primary duty was Naval Special Warfare Communications Technician, as Leading Petty Officer. Collateral duties were Communications Security Manager, Magnetometer, and Safety Officer. Navy and Marine Corps Achievement Medals dated 2005 and 2006, and the Global War on Terrorism Service Medal dated 2005, are recorded for that command. The DD-214 records 6 years, 4 months, 24 days of sea service and 3 years, 1 month, 13 days of foreign service.

About six months in Iraq in 2006 were with Combined Joint Task Force Troy. The recorded specialty remained Naval Special Warfare Communications Technician. The Army Achievement Medal dated 2006 is recorded for that deployment, and the Iraq Campaign Medal star for it is the National Resolution phase. The individual-augmentee tour that began in October 2008 was a volunteer assignment: five months at Fort Bliss with Headquarters and Headquarters Battery, 3rd Battalion, 3rd Air Defense Artillery Regiment, then the remainder in country at Joint Base Balad with Task Force Iron Shield in 2009. The primary duty was Counter Rocket, Artillery, and Mortar, as Leading Petty Officer. Collateral duties were Sergeant of the Guard and Mount Watch. The Army Commendation Medal dated 2009 is recorded for this tour, and the Iraq Campaign Medal star for it is the Iraq Sovereignty phase. Orders to Joint Communications Support Element at MacDill Air Force Base followed that year. The primary duty there was Team Chief in 3rd Squadron, as a Naval Special Warfare Communications Technician. Joint Service Commendation Medals in 2013 and December 2014, a Joint Service Achievement Medal in 2013, and three Joint Meritorious Unit Awards are recorded for that command. Deployments from there ran about seven months to Afghanistan in 2010–2011 in support of Combined Joint Special Operations Task Force — Afghanistan and Operational Detachment Alpha 3213 of 3rd Special Forces Group (Airborne). The duty was communications, as Team Chief. {{redacted-2011}} The Army Commendation Medal dated 2011 is recorded for that deployment, and that phase is the first star on the Afghanistan Campaign Medal. About seven months in 2012–2013 were in support of Special Operations Joint Task Force — Afghanistan and the 75th Ranger Regiment, again as Team Chief in communications. The NATO Medal dated 2013 is for the International Security Assistance Force, and that phase is the second star on the Afghanistan Campaign Medal. Enlisted Expeditionary Warfare Specialist sits on that expeditionary stretch. The last command was Naval Hospital Jacksonville, as Leading Chief Petty Officer. The Navy and Marine Corps Commendation Medal dated 2018 is recorded for that command.

Service ran under four presidents, {{b}}William J. Clinton{{/b}}, {{b}}George W. Bush{{/b}}, {{b}}Barack Obama{{/b}}, and {{b}}Donald J. Trump{{/b}}, through four campaign phases of the Global War on Terrorism, across two theaters of operations, Iraq and Afghanistan, and two warfare domains, maritime and land. His two decades of service reflect great credit upon himself and are in keeping with the highest traditions of the naval service.

In this century, as in the last, the words of wartime veteran President {{b}}John F. Kennedy{{/b}} ring true. On 1 August 1963, at the United States Naval Academy, he said,

{{quote}}“Any man who may be asked in this century what he did to make his life worth while, I think can respond with a good deal of pride and satisfaction: ‘I served in the United States Navy.’”`,pathTitle:`The same years, one line`,pathLead:`Rank, assignments, and deployments share one axis. Places below follows the Map order. A bar opens the explanation.`,eventsNote:`The last track is public history during these years. It is not part of the service record.`,footerNav:`More`,pageNav:`Pages`,prevPage:`Previous`,nextPage:`Next`,language:`Language`,english:`English`,portuguese:`Português`,theme:`Theme`,light:`Light`,dark:`Dark`,system:`System`,settingsTitle:`Settings`,settingsLead:`Language changes the menus and buttons. The service record stays in English until the Portuguese record is finished, and official names stay as written.`,schoolsLead:`Courses in the order they were recorded. Open a row for the explanation, the place, and what the course was for.`,commandsLead:`The six commands. Each row is the crest, the rating badge on the way in, loose ribbons, and the rating badge on the way out. Slide left for in-theater commands, sponsors, partners, customers, and exercises.`,necsLead:`Navy Enlisted Classifications in the order awarded. A code that later changed shows the current code. Open a row for the criteria.`,logbookLead:`Scroll the assigned commands. The uniform plate, ribbon rack, map pin, supporting units, and admin facts follow each command. Labels and dates come from the record — blank beats a guess.`},Jr={title:`Shadowbox`,home:`Início`,search:`Buscar`,searchPlaceholder:`Buscar no registro`,menu:`Menu`,noMatch:`Nada encontrado no registro.`,timeline:`Linha do tempo`,logbook:`Diário de bordo`,admin:`Admin`,commands:`Comandos`,uniforms:`Uniformes`,decorations:`Condecorações`,onduty:`Em serviço`,offduty:`Fora de serviço`,ops:`Operações`,map:`Mapa`,schools:`Escolas`,necs:`NECs`,dateNeeded:`Data em aberto`,sources:`Fontes`,contact:`Contato`,guestbook:`Livro de visitas`,memories:`Memórias`,settings:`Ajustes`,close:`Fechar`,made:`Feito por Mim & Grok, 2026`,bio:`Chief Schickler alistou-se em Miami em 30 de junho de 1997 como Seaman e aposentou-se em 28 de fevereiro de 2018 como Electronics Technician, após 20 anos, 8 meses e 1 dia. Não houve serviço nos dois postos de aprendiz.

Sua progressão nos graus de petty officer foi rápida. Petty Officer Third Class em dezembro de 1998, Petty Officer Second Class em maio de 1999, e Petty Officer First Class em junho de 2003. Petty Officer Second Class veio em menos de dois anos de serviço, quando as médias da Marinha da época colocavam o E-5 um pouco além dos cinco anos. Petty Officer First Class veio aos seis anos, quando essas médias colocavam o E-6 além dos onze, e quando o percurso de Electronics Technician ainda indica cerca de nove anos até esse grau. A seleção para chief petty officer veio do Joint Communications Support Element, com data de posto em 16 de setembro de 2014, no último ano dessa comissão.

Quase metade da carreira foi no ultramar. De 2001 a 2007 o serviço foi em Guam. No USS Frank Cable (AS-40) a função principal foi Fleet Electronics Calibration Technician, como Leading Petty Officer, e a função colateral foi Safety. O Enlisted Surface Warfare Specialist foi ganho nesse navio. Três Navy E Ribbons, de 2001, 2002 e 2003, constam desse navio, junto com a Humanitarian Service Medal pelo Typhoon Pongsona, datada de 2003, e a Navy Rifle Marksmanship Ribbon em Expert, registrada em 2001. No Explosive Ordnance Disposal Mobile Unit Five a função principal foi Naval Special Warfare Communications Technician, como Leading Petty Officer. As funções colaterais foram Communications Security Manager, Magnetometer e Safety Officer. Navy and Marine Corps Achievement Medals datadas de 2005 e 2006, e a Global War on Terrorism Service Medal datada de 2005, constam desse comando. O DD-214 registra 6 anos, 4 meses e 24 dias de serviço no mar e 3 anos, 1 mês e 13 dias de serviço no estrangeiro.

Cerca de seis meses no Iraque, em 2006, foram com a Combined Joint Task Force Troy. A especialidade registrada continuou Naval Special Warfare Communications Technician. A Army Achievement Medal datada de 2006 consta desse destacamento, e a estrela da Iraq Campaign Medal dessa fase é National Resolution. A comissão de individual augmentee que começou em outubro de 2008 foi voluntária: cinco meses em Fort Bliss com o Headquarters and Headquarters Battery, 3rd Battalion, 3rd Air Defense Artillery Regiment, e o restante no teatro, em Joint Base Balad, com a Task Force Iron Shield em 2009. A função principal foi Counter Rocket, Artillery, and Mortar, como Leading Petty Officer. As funções colaterais foram Sergeant of the Guard e Mount Watch. A Army Commendation Medal datada de 2009 consta dessa comissão, e a estrela da Iraq Campaign Medal dessa fase é Iraq Sovereignty. As ordens para o Joint Communications Support Element, em MacDill Air Force Base, vieram nesse mesmo ano. A função principal lá foi Team Chief no 3rd Squadron, como Naval Special Warfare Communications Technician. Joint Service Commendation Medals em 2013 e em dezembro de 2014, uma Joint Service Achievement Medal em 2013, e três Joint Meritorious Unit Awards constam desse comando. Os destacamentos de lá duraram cerca de sete meses no Afeganistão em 2010–2011, em apoio à Combined Joint Special Operations Task Force — Afghanistan e ao Operational Detachment Alpha 3213 do 3rd Special Forces Group (Airborne). A função foi comunicações, como Team Chief. {{redacted-2011}} A Army Commendation Medal datada de 2011 consta desse destacamento, e essa fase é a primeira estrela da Afghanistan Campaign Medal. Cerca de sete meses em 2012–2013 foram em apoio à Special Operations Joint Task Force — Afghanistan e ao 75th Ranger Regiment, de novo como Team Chief em comunicações. A NATO Medal datada de 2013 é pela International Security Assistance Force, e essa fase é a segunda estrela da Afghanistan Campaign Medal. O Enlisted Expeditionary Warfare Specialist fica nesse trecho expedicionário. O último comando foi o Naval Hospital Jacksonville, como Leading Chief Petty Officer. A Navy and Marine Corps Commendation Medal datada de 2018 consta desse comando.

O serviço correu sob quatro presidentes, {{b}}William J. Clinton{{/b}}, {{b}}George W. Bush{{/b}}, {{b}}Barack Obama{{/b}} e {{b}}Donald J. Trump{{/b}}, por quatro fases de campanha da Global War on Terrorism, em dois teatros de operações, Iraque e Afeganistão, e em dois domínios de guerra, o marítimo e o terrestre. Suas duas décadas de serviço refletem grande crédito sobre ele e estão em consonância com as mais altas tradições do serviço naval.

Neste século, como no anterior, soam verdadeiras as palavras do Presidente {{b}}John F. Kennedy{{/b}}, veterano de guerra. Em 1 de agosto de 1963, na United States Naval Academy, ele disse:

{{quote}}“Any man who may be asked in this century what he did to make his life worth while, I think can respond with a good deal of pride and satisfaction: ‘I served in the United States Navy.’”`,pathTitle:`Os mesmos anos, numa linha`,pathLead:`Posto, funções e deslocamentos partilham um eixo. Uma barra abre a explicação.`,eventsNote:`A última faixa é história pública destes anos. Não faz parte do registro de serviço.`,footerNav:`Mais`,pageNav:`Páginas`,prevPage:`Anterior`,nextPage:`Seguinte`,language:`Idioma`,english:`English`,portuguese:`Português`,theme:`Tema`,light:`Claro`,dark:`Escuro`,system:`Sistema`,settingsTitle:`Ajustes`,settingsLead:`O idioma muda os menus e os botões. O registro de serviço continua em inglês até o registro em português ficar pronto, e os nomes oficiais ficam como foram escritos.`,schoolsLead:`Cursos na ordem em que foram registrados. Abra uma linha para a explicação, o lugar e para que servia o curso.`,commandsLead:`Os seis comandos. Cada fileira é o distintivo, o distintivo de posto na entrada, fitas soltas, e o distintivo de posto na saída. Deslize para o comando no teatro, patrocinadores, parceiros, clientes e exercícios.`,necsLead:`Classificações de praça (NEC) na ordem em que foram concedidas. Código que mudou mostra o código atual. Abra a linha para o critério.`,logbookLead:`Role os comandos atribuídos. O uniforme, o rack, o pino no mapa, as unidades e o admin acompanham cada comando. Rótulos e datas vêm do registro — em branco vale mais que um palpite.`};function Yr(e){return e===`pt`?Jr:qr}function Xr(){if(typeof window>`u`||typeof navigator<`u`&&navigator.onLine===!1||window.parent!==window)return!1;let e=window.location.hostname.toLowerCase();return!(e===`grok-sandbox.com`||e.endsWith(`.grok-sandbox.com`))}var Zr=`https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Quicksand:wght@500;600&family=Source+Serif+4:ital,opsz,wght@0,8..60,400;0,8..60,500;0,8..60,600;1,8..60,400&family=Source+Sans+3:ital,wght@0,400;0,600;1,400&display=swap`;func